(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-1-pharm-drill-dermatology"]={"title":"Dermatology Medications Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which acne treatment bleaches hair and clothing?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Azelaic acid","Tretinoin","Clindamycin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It bleaches hair and clothes — the practical warning every patient needs before the first application. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which topical acne agent can cause hypopigmentation?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Azelaic acid","Adapalene","Erythromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hypopigmentation alongside mild irritation and dryness. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which oral acne drug causes retinoid dermatitis, photophobia and raised serum lipids?","choices":["Doxycycline","Spironolactone","Isotretinoin","Benzoyl peroxide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Retinoid dermatitis, photophobia, arthralgia, headache, alopecia, brittle nails and raised lipids. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which class must be avoided in pregnancy and causes photosensitivity and severe sunburn?","choices":["Benzoyl peroxide","Azelaic acid","Bacitracin","Topical retinoids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tretinoin, adapalene and tazarotene — photosensitivity, severe sunburn, and avoided in pregnancy. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"The erythema, desquamation, burning and stinging from topical retinoids do what over time?","choices":["Decrease with time and with emollients","Worsen steadily until the drug is stopped","Are unaffected by emollients","Indicate an allergic reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. They settle — which is exactly what the patient must be told, or they stop the drug in week two. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic is preferred for acne, though it is losing efficacy to resistance?","choices":["Erythromycin","Clindamycin","Mupirocin","Bacitracin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The preferred topical antibiotic, with efficacy falling over time because of Propionibacterium acnes resistance. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which drugs are used as antiandrogens for acne in some women?","choices":["Isotretinoin","Doxycycline","Spironolactone","Adapalene"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Useful in some women, working on the hormonal driver rather than the follicle. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Local tissue atrophy and adrenal suppression, with the need to taper to prevent a flare, belong to which acne treatment?","choices":["Topical retinoids","Benzoyl peroxide","Azelaic acid","Corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atrophy locally, adrenal suppression systemically, and tapering to prevent rebound. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Skin atrophy, acne, rosacea and allergic dermatitis are the LOCAL effects of which class?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Topical retinoids","Topical immunomodulators","Topical antifungals"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The local list. Systemically they can suppress the adrenal axis, raise glucose, and cause glaucoma, cataracts and growth retardation in children. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Glaucoma, cataracts and growth retardation in children are SYSTEMIC risks of which class?","choices":["Topical antifungals","Topical corticosteroids","Benzoyl peroxide","Mupirocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Absorbed steroid produces systemic effects — which is why potency and duration are limited, especially in children. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which agents are second-line for atopic dermatitis, after topical steroids, and cause a burning sensation?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Topical retinoids","Tacrolimus","Imiquimod"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Topical immunomodulators, second line, with burning on application — and avoided in patients with weakened immune systems. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which topical class must be avoided in patients with weakened immune systems?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Topical antifungals","Benzoyl peroxide","Topical immunomodulators"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tacrolimus and pimecrolimus suppress local immunity, so they are avoided where immunity is already impaired. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic has no systemic toxicity and only rarely causes allergic dermatitis?","choices":["Bacitracin","Neomycin","Polymyxin B","Mupirocin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rare allergic dermatitis, no systemic toxicity. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which tetracycline restriction matters most when prescribing for skin disease?","choices":["Contraindicated in renal failure and dialysis","Contraindicated under 8 years old and in pregnancy","Contraindicated with dairy and antacids only","Contraindicated over 65 years of age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Contraindicated in children under eight and in pregnant women — the same rule that governs systemic use. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which acne agent is used when resistance has made a topical antibiotic less useful, and is often combined with it?","choices":["Isotretinoin","Spironolactone","Benzoyl peroxide","Tacrolimus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Combining it with a topical antibiotic slows the resistance that is eroding clindamycin and erythromycin. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which agent causes skin irritation in virtually every patient who uses it?","choices":["Bacitracin","Mupirocin","Nystatin","Imiquimod"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Irritation in virtually all patients — expected, and not a reason to stop. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which topical antifungal is for candidal infection specifically?","choices":["Nystatin","Tolnaftate","Terbinafine","Ciclopirox"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For candidal infection — tolnaftate in the same pairing has NO candidal activity. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which topical drug is applied as a lacquer to the nail?","choices":["Terbinafine","Ciclopirox","Nystatin","Mupirocin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Penlac nail lacquer, for onychomycosis. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which class treats athlete's foot, ringworm and jock itch — the superficial dermatophytes?","choices":["Nystatin","Bacitracin","Allylamines","Imiquimod"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Terbinafine and naftifine, oral or topical. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which topical antibiotic can accumulate systemically if used over a large area?","choices":["Bacitracin","Mupirocin","Clindamycin","Neomycin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic accumulation, on top of frequent sensitisation. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which agents are used topically for recurrent cold sores?","choices":["Penciclovir","Imiquimod","Mupirocin","Tacrolimus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Topical antivirals for recurrent orolabial herpes simplex. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which topical antifungals are used for vulvovaginal candidiasis?","choices":["Tolnaftate","Topical azoles","Terbinafine","Bacitracin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clotrimazole, miconazole and sertaconazole, topically and vaginally. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the most important thing to tell a patient starting a topical retinoid about the sun?","choices":["It protects against sunburn","It has no interaction with sun exposure","It causes photosensitivity and severe sunburn","It should only be applied in the morning"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Photosensitivity and severe sunburn — so sun protection is part of the prescription. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which topical antibacterial must be kept off open or denuded skin at high dose?","choices":["Bacitracin","Mupirocin","Clindamycin","Polymyxin B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Because of neurotoxicity and nephrotoxicity if enough is absorbed. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which drug is applied inside the nose rather than to a lesion?","choices":["Mupirocin","Bacitracin","Imiquimod","Ciclopirox"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. To eliminate nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus, MRSA included. — 02. Dermatology Medications(1).pptx, Slide 47"}]};
